seg-a179dc97d338 = What it writes

# text
# c1: <dir>/<source>/<sequence>.obs.jsonl
# c2: kshana obs check
# c3: kshana obs convert
# c4: kshana obs live
seg-87fe5aaf19f2 = <c1/>. Every file starts with the source's header record, so each is a valid observation file on its own (<c2/> passes on each). The epochs of a connection are the epochs a replay of the same bytes gives (<c3/>), bit for bit, from the same live reader <c4/> uses. A stream that ends or fails is opened again every second; the time without input is counted as a gap and never filled, and each new connection starts a new file.

# text
seg-ad856ad710eb = Status

# text
# c1: GET /health
# c2: ok
# c3: GET /status
# c4: kshana.station-status/1
seg-d3f8d695280e = <c1/> answers <c2/>. <c3/> answers <c4/>: the station, the engine version, uptime, and per source whether it is connected, its connections and gaps, bytes and epochs read, when the last epoch arrived, the last error and the current file; and the last fleet report's outcome.

