seg-bcdbaf6b52ad = Once a second is enough. The other <c1/> values (<c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/>, ...) read the messages listed in <a1>OBS-SCHEMA.md</a1>.

# text
seg-5aab15bb6ba2 = { "2" }. Connect it

# text
# c1: /dev/ttyACM0
# c2: /dev/ttyUSB0
# c3: /dev/serial/by-id/
seg-f825a5f47f37 = <b>USB.</b> Most receivers appear as <c1/> or <c2/>. Use the stable name under <c3/> in the configuration, because the numbers change when devices are plugged in a different order.

# text
# c1: stty -F /dev/serial/by-id/<receiver> 115200 raw -echo
seg-89c4a3b240c3 = <b>Serial line.</b> The Station does not set the line speed. Set it and raw mode before the Station opens the port: <c1/>. Use the speed the receiver sends at. A USB receiver that presents a virtual serial port ignores the speed.

# text
# c1: input = "tcp:<address>:<port>"
seg-b5d3340309de = <b>Network.</b> A receiver or a serial-to-network server (for example ser2net) that serves the stream on a TCP port is read with <c1/>. The Station reconnects every second when the connection drops.

# text
# c1: cat /dev/serial/by-id/<receiver> | head -c 200 | od -c
# c2: µb
# c3: $@
# c4: $G
seg-17e6345fda27 = Check that bytes arrive before going further: <c1/> shows the sync bytes (<c2/> for UBX, <c3/> for SBF, <c4/> for NMEA).

# text
seg-b2a1c6198be5 = { "3" }. Write the configuration

# text
# c1: ProtectSystem=strict
# c2: /var/lib/kshana-station
seg-e85c0c9dfbae = The service runs with a read-only system (<c1/>) and may write only under <c2/>, so keep the configuration and the output there:

# text
# c1: id
# c2: lat_deg
# c3: lon_deg
# c4: height_m
# c5: [[source]]
# c6: input
# c7: tcp:
# c8: format
# c9: [output] dir
seg-a29743641a5a = Set the station's <c1/>, its position (<c2/>, <c3/>, <c4/>) and its antenna, and one <c5/> per receiver, with <c6/> the device or <c7/> address from step { "2" } and <c8/> from step { "1" }. Keep <c9/> relative (it is taken relative to the configuration file).

# text
# c1: rotate_mb × keep
# c2: keep
seg-b9a870581181 = The output needs <c1/> of disk per source: { "3" } GiB with the defaults ({ "64" } MiB × { "48" } files). The oldest file goes first when a source has <c2/> files.

